          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c is 58.",
          "answer": "A value of 58 only makes sense once you know its unit. Many labs in some countries report HbA1c in mmol/mol, and others report a percentage. Check your lab report or ask the lab which unit is used.\n\nI could not find an mmol/mol-to-percent conversion in the sources I searched, so I can't give you the equivalent percentage or say for certain which category 58 falls into. The guidance I found sets its thresholds in percentages: an HbA1c of 5.7 to 6.4 percent is classed as prediabetes, and 6.5 percent or above meets the diagnostic threshold for diabetes (Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds). Those figures are not directly comparable to 58 until the value is converted, and I would rather not guess the conversion.\n\nThe same guidance says a diagnosis is normally confirmed by a second test, unless symptoms are clear. A single result is therefore a starting point for discussion, not a final answer.\n\nPlease take your result, including its unit and reference range, to your clinician. They can tell you what it means for you and whether any follow-up is needed.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
